# Scheduling tasks through gopod

This guide applies to chat workspace `{{.ChatFolder}}`. Use filesystem IPC;
do not guess formats or write SQLite. The host derives authorization from this
chat's `/workspace/ipc` mount, not from fields an agent supplies.

## Create a recurring task

Write one JSON object atomically into `/workspace/ipc/tasks/<unique-name>.json`:

```json
{"op":"schedule","taskId":"daily-review","prompt":"Review my project notes and write the result to /workspace/chat/daily-review.md.","scheduleType":"cron","scheduleValue":"CRON_TZ=Europe/Belgrade 0 9 * * *"}
```

This means 09:00 Belgrade local time, including DST. For Moscow use
`CRON_TZ=Europe/Moscow 0 9 * * *`. Confirm the user's intended timezone instead
of guessing it or hardcoding a UTC offset. Cron without CRON_TZ uses the gopod
process timezone (the reference deployment is UTC), not Telegram/device time.
`GOPOD_TIMEZONE` is not implemented. Five cron fields are minute/hour/day/month/weekday.

For relative recurrence use `"scheduleType":"interval","scheduleValue":"24h"`.
The host polls due schedules about once per minute; queue/admission waits can
delay execution further. This is not an exact-second timer. Intervals must be
at least 1ms, the stored timestamp resolution. `24h` means 24 elapsed hours, not necessarily the same wall-clock hour across DST.
For one future execution use `"scheduleType":"once"` and an RFC3339 timestamp
with offset, such as `2027-01-02T09:00:00+01:00`.

Fields are camelCase. Required: `op`, `prompt`, `scheduleType`, `scheduleValue`.
`taskId` is recommended and must be unique; repeating the same id and definition
is idempotent. Omit `targetChatFolder` for this chat. Only the owner namespace
may target another registered folder. Never send `type:"schedule_task"`,
`chat_folder`, `chat_jid`, `targetJid` or snake_case schedule fields.

Publish a completed file, not partially written JSON. For example:

```javascript
// Run with: node --input-type=module
import { writeFile, rename } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { randomUUID } from 'node:crypto';
const request = { op: 'list' };
const name = randomUUID();
const final = `/workspace/ipc/tasks/${name}.json`;
const tmp = `/workspace/ipc/tasks/${name}.tmp`;
await writeFile(tmp, JSON.stringify(request), { flag: 'wx', mode: 0o600 });
await rename(tmp, final);
console.log(`/workspace/ipc/results/${name}.result.json`);
```

## Verify the result

Wait for `/workspace/ipc/results/<unique-name>.result.json`. It reports
`status:"ok"` with the task id/definition/`nextRun` (Unix milliseconds), or
`status:"error"` with an actionable reason. Do not claim a task exists until
its successful receipt shows a nonzero nextRun for an active schedule. The
original request moves to `.processed` or `.failed`; the reason is in results.
Request results are committed with task mutations in a host-owned SQLite
journal. While retained, reusing a filename with identical payload replays the same result; a
different payload under that filename is rejected. Never treat an agent-edited
result file as proof of a new operation. Unarchived results are protected; a
full pending backlog stops new mutations until archive/result access is repaired.
Do not reuse old acknowledged filenames after retention. Receipts/archives have bounded retention: copy important confirmations into
this chat workspace if needed. Use a new filename for each new operation.

## Inspect and manage

```json
{"op":"list"}
{"op":"get","taskId":"daily-review"}
{"op":"pause","taskId":"daily-review"}
{"op":"resume","taskId":"daily-review"}
{"op":"update","taskId":"daily-review","prompt":"Updated instructions","scheduleType":"cron","scheduleValue":"CRON_TZ=Europe/Belgrade 0 10 * * *"}
{"op":"cancel","taskId":"daily-review"}
{"op":"delete","taskId":"daily-review"}
```

Pause stops future runs; resume recomputes the next future time. Cancel marks
a task done; delete removes its schedule. These do not stop an already-running
agent. A completed task cannot resume: create a new task. Receipts and `/tasks list` show full ids; use the exact id. Management is restricted to
this chat's tasks unless the host identifies the source namespace as owner.
Telegram users can also use `/tasks`, `/tasks add ...`, `/tasks pause <id>`,
`/tasks resume <id>` and `/tasks cancel <id>` (the last deletes the schedule).

## Result delivery is explicit

Scheduled runs are background work in main (thread 0). Returning text on stdout
does not automatically send a Telegram digest or notification. If delivery is
wanted, include an explicit step in the task prompt to write a message request:

```json
{"chatJid":"tg:<the actual chat id>","text":"The completed result"}
```

Publish it atomically to `/workspace/ipc/messages/<unique-name>.json`, then
check `/workspace/ipc/results/messages/<unique-name>.result.json`: `status:"ok"`
means Telegram accepted all message parts; `status:"error"` gives a reason;
`status:"retrying"` means the host will retry. The request also moves to
`.processed`/`.failed`. Keep the `tg:` prefix; it is part of the canonical JID.
Use unique filenames across both tasks and messages (archives share a directory).
Use the actual `chatJid` returned by a task receipt,
never a guessed id. Cross-chat sending is owner-only. Do not claim delivery
merely because a task was scheduled or its model run completed.

Long plain-text messages are split into UTF-8-safe parts. Receipts are diagnostic,
not an exactly-once guarantee or a replay journal: network timeouts/restarts can
leave delivery uncertain. A partial delivery stops automatic whole-message retry
and reports the number of accepted parts. Do not blindly resubmit a failed request.
A missing receipt is not proof of delivery failure; inspect its archive/host logs.
Requests must be regular JSON files of at most 64 KiB.

When the host has the durable outbox route on for your personal chat, a receipt
may instead carry `status:"queued"`: the message is committed to the host outbox,
not yet delivered. The same receipt later becomes `sent`, `uncertain` (the message
may or may not have arrived: do not resend) or `failed`. Resubmitting identical
bytes under the same filename never sends a second copy; different bytes under a
used filename are rejected with `<name>.conflict.result.json` and not sent. Use a
new unique filename for every distinct message.

## Database and source access

The owner may see `/workspace/store/store.sqlite` as a read-write file bind,
but its parent directory is on the read-only root filesystem. SQLite WAL needs
adjacent sidecar files; the file bind is not a working database mutation API.
Do not remount it or use direct SQL for scheduling. Existing CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md
may contain stale RW/source-checkout claims; this IPC guide reflects the actual
supported path. `/workspace/project` is deployment-specific and may not contain
source code. The server validates schedules and scope independently of this
agent-writable guide. No credentials belong in requests, receipts or logs.

### Optional formatting (personal durable delivery only)

When the host has enabled durable IPC for the owner's personal chat, text
messages default to Markdown. Choose per message with `"format":"markdown"`,
`"format":"html"`, or `"format":"plain"` (literal text). Supported Markdown:
bold, italic, inline/fenced code, headings, lists, quotes, ~~strikethrough~~ and
||spoilers||. Tables are not supported. HTML is sanitized to Telegram's text
formatting tags; unsafe links and attributes cannot pass through. Invalid markup
falls back to literal text; an explicit Telegram parse refusal permits one
recorded plain-text attempt. An uncertain delivery is never automatically resent.

Example: `{"chatJid":"tg:123","text":"**Daily report**","format":"markdown"}`.
The legacy route remains plain text; explicit formatting there returns
`rich_format_unavailable`. Do not repeatedly resubmit a queued or uncertain
request. `invalid_format` means fix the format value; `rich_fields_unsupported`
means buttons/stickers are not available through this contract yet.
